---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const FourSegments = `PROCEDURE (i)    the procedure itself
                 its name, its parameters, and an algorithm
                 with sequencing, selection and iteration
                 in its body

PROCEDURE (ii)   where you call it

LIST (i)         where data goes INTO the list

LIST (ii)        where that same list's data gets USED`;

const WithComments = `// works out the total for one tier
// tiers[] and prices[] stay in the same order
function priceFor(tier) {
  let total = 0;              // running sum

  for (let i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
    if (tiers[i] === tier) {
      total = total + prices[i];
    }
  }

  return total;
}`;

const WithoutComments = `function priceFor(tier) {
  let total = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
    if (tiers[i] === tier) {
      total = total + prices[i];
    }
  }

  return total;
}`;

const Rules = `THE PROJECT REFERENCE

  no comments        anywhere, in any segment
  no course content  no assignment text, no notes
  at least 10 pt     readable
  not blurry

  Comments or course content here = ZERO
  on the whole Create Task, including the four
  written responses.`;

const SelfTest = `Cover your program. Look ONLY at your four segments.

  WR1     Who is this for, and how does the design
          meet their need?                      ____
  WR2(a)  How many times does the first loop in
          Procedure (i) run?                    ____
          What would make it run forever?       ____
  WR2(b)  A change to that procedure causing a
          run-time error, and why?              ____
  WR2(c)  Using LIST (i)'s list -- count the
          elements equal to a value.            ____

Any blank means the wrong code is in your reference.
Today you can still change it.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.4: The Personalized Project Reference</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>Session 63</h2>

          <p>
            <b>No building today.</b> Today you make the four-page document you take
            into the exam in May.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Four of your six points are answered from this
            document and nothing else.</b> Not from your program &mdash; you will not
            have your program. From four code segments on a desk.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>You have done this once</b>, as the practice reference at Build 2. The
            rules are the same and this time they count.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.4</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">The Personalized Project Reference</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Four Segments</p>
              <p>Comments Are Documentation</p>
              <p>Choosing Code You Can Talk About</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What It Is</h2>
              <Code code={FourSegments} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Four Code Segments" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">That is all of it. Four pieces of your own program.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Rules</h2>
              <Code code={Rules} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Read Twice" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Comments Are Required. And Forbidden.</h2>
              <p><b>Component A</b> &mdash; your program code PDF &mdash; must include your comments.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Component C</b> &mdash; this reference &mdash; must have none.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Same code. Submitted twice. One version stripped.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>That Is Not a Contradiction</h2>
              <p>Comments are <b class="text-sn-theme">program documentation</b> &mdash; written for people, ignored by the computer.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Your code PDF is judged partly on whether you credited your sources. Comments are where you do that.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The reference is a memory aid for <i>you</i>. Notes in it would be notes into an exam.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Documentation Is More Than Comments</h2>
              <p>A written description of what a piece of code does and how it was built.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Comments are one form. <b>Your journal is another.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Some environments have no comments at all &mdash; and then you document some other way.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Strip Them Carefully</h2>
              <Code code={WithComments} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Component A -- comments in" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Like This</h2>
              <Code code={WithoutComments} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Component C -- comments out" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Three gone, including the one at the end of a line.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The One People Miss</h2>
              <p>The comment on the <b>same line as code</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><code>let total = 0;   // running sum</code></p>
              <p class="fragment">It does not look like a comment when you are scanning for whole lines.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Now the Harder Question</h2>
              <p>Which four segments do you choose?</p>
              <p class="fragment">The instinct is: the best-looking code.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">The right answer is: the code you can answer four questions about.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>So Test It Before You Submit</h2>
              <p>Cover your program. Look only at your four segments.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Answer all four prompts.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b>A blank means you picked the wrong code</b> &mdash; and today is
                the last day you can pick different code.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Deadline</h2>
              <p>Submit all three components as <b>final</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">Miss the reference deadline and you do not get it on exam day.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Four questions about your own program, from memory, with nothing in front of you.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And You Cannot Revise</h2>
              <p>Once a component is submitted as final, that is the version scored.</p>
              <p class="fragment">There is no fixing it later. Check before you press it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Assembling the four required code segments</li>
                <li>Explaining what program documentation is and what comments are for</li>
                <li>Removing every comment, including in-line ones</li>
                <li>Choosing segments by whether they can be written about</li>
                <li>Testing the reference against all four written-response prompts</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Program documentation</dt>
                  <dd>A written description of what code does and how it was developed.</dd>
                  <dt>Comment</dt>
                  <dd>Documentation written into the program, read by people, with no effect on how the program runs.</dd>
                  <dt>Submitted as final</dt>
                  <dd>The state a component must reach to be scored. Cannot be revised afterward.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Choose the Four Segments</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Find each of the four in your program and note where it is.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Procedure (i) must contain a parameter that affects what the
              procedure does</b>, and an algorithm with sequencing, selection and
              iteration in its body.
            </li>
            <li><b>List (ii) must show the list being <i>used</i></b> &mdash; new data made from it, or more than one element read. Storing it is List (i)'s job.</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Strip Every Comment</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Whole-line comments, and <b>comments at the end of a line of code</b>.</li>
            <li>Then read all four segments once more, slowly, looking only for comments.</li>
            <li><b>Then have someone else read them for the same thing.</b> They may not help you write it; they may certainly tell you a comment is still there.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Keep your commented version.</b> Component A needs it. You are making a
            second copy, not editing the original.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: The Self-Test</h3>

          <Code code={SelfTest} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Can You Answer From the Reference Alone?" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Do this before you submit anything.</b> A reference that cannot answer
            the four prompts is worth two points instead of six, and the only day you
            can change it is today.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The College Board recommends exactly this
            check</b> &mdash; practicing a set of written responses to confirm the code
            in your reference is enough to answer them.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Make the PDF</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><b>At least 10 pt. Not blurry.</b></li>
            <li>Nothing on the page but your code &mdash; no headings copied from an assignment, no notes to yourself.</li>
            <li>Read it once as a picture rather than as code, checking for anything that is not program code.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Four segments, correctly chosen</li>
            <li><b>Zero comments</b>, checked twice and by someone else</li>
            <li>All four prompts answerable from the reference alone</li>
            <li>A readable PDF, 10 pt or larger</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Do not submit as final until session 64</b>, when all three components
            go together. But it must be <i>ready</i> today.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your finished Project Reference, and your Task 3 self-test with all five
            lines filled in.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
